mariosgit: what does this mean
Linking .pio/build/stm32f4/firmware.elf
/Users/mario/.platformio/packages/toolchain-gccarmnoneeabi/bin/../lib/gcc/arm-none-eabi/7.2.1/../../../../arm-none-eabi/bin/ld: error: .pio/build/stm32f4/firmware.elf uses VFP register arguments, lib/IntelRDFPMathLib20U1/libgcc111libbid-arm.a(bid128_acos.o) does not
/Users/mario/.platformio/packages/toolchain-gccarmnoneeabi/bin/../lib/gcc/arm-none-eabi/7.2.1/../../../../arm-none-eabi/bin/ld: failed to merge target specific data of file lib/IntelRDFPMathLib20U1/libgcc111libbid-arm.a(bid128_acos.o)

GitHub Copilot: Completed (3/6) *Fix linker library path for BID archive*

Seeing that linker diagnostic tells me the prebuilt BID archive was compiled with the soft-float (non-VFP) ABI, while your STM32F4 firmware objects are using the hard-float/VFP calling convention. The two ABIs pack floating-point arguments differently, so the linker refuses to combine them.

## What it implies
- `firmware.elf` (your code) was built with something like `-mfpu=fpv4-sp-d16 -mfloat-abi=hard` (the default for Cortex-M4F in PlatformIO), meaning any function taking floats passes them in VFP registers.
- `libgcc111libbid-arm.a` was built without VFP support (`-mfloat-abi=soft` or `softfp`), so its object files expect all float arguments in core registers/stack.
- When the linker tries to glue both worlds together, it reports “failed to merge target specific data” because those conventions are incompatible.

## How you can resolve it
You need both the archive and your code to agree on the float ABI:

- **Rebuild the BID archive for hard-float:** If you can regenerate `gcc111libbid-arm.a`, compile it with the same flags you use for the firmware (e.g. `-mcpu=cortex-m4 -mfpu=fpv4-sp-d16 -mfloat-abi=hard`). That keeps hardware FPU enabled everywhere and is usually the best option performance-wise.
- **Or switch the firmware to match the archive:** Add `-mfloat-abi=softfp` (or `soft`) and drop the `-mfpu` flag in `[env:stm32f4].build_flags` so your objects no longer use VFP registers. The linker error will vanish, but every floating-point call—including to the BID library—will route through software helpers, so expect a speed hit.
- Mixing the two ABIs isn’t supported—one side has to be rebuilt.

Let me know which direction you prefer and I’ll help wire the flags (or set up a recompile) accordingly.

