-arduinoststm32/variants/NUCLEO_F411RE /Users/mario/.platformio/packages/framework-arduinoststm32/cores/arduino/stm32/timer.c

GitHub Copilot: You’ll want to rebuild the BID archive with exactly the same architecture switches your STM32 firmware is using so the ABIs match:

- `-mcpu=cortex-m4`
- `-mfpu=fpv4-sp-d16`
- `-mfloat-abi=hard`
- `-mthumb`

Those are the critical ones that define the calling convention. You can keep the other switches (`-ffunction-sections`, `-fdata-sections`, `-Os`, etc.) if you’re chasing size/speed, but they don’t affect ABI compatibility.

Recompile each object in the BID library with those flags (ideally using the same toolchain version you’re using here) and rebuild the archive; once that’s done, the linker will stop complaining about mismatched VFP register usage.

mariosgit: which -O does minimize the size (and max speed)

GitHub Copilot: Given those STM32 build flags, you’ve already got `-Os`, which is GCC’s “optimize for size” preset. It keeps most speed-oriented passes from `-O2` but disables the ones that typically bloat code, so it’s the right choice when flash is tight yet you still want decent performance.

If you can spare a bit more flash and want a little more throughput, try `-O2`. It enables extra loop/vector optimizations that often help compute-heavy code, at the cost of a larger binary.

`-O3` adds even more aggressive vectorization and unrolling; on Cortex-M MCUs it rarely pays off—you tend to gain only a few percent while eating noticeably more flash and sometimes stack.

`-Ofast` is like `-O3` plus math “relaxations” (e.g., it may ignore IEEE corner cases). It can break decimal-math correctness expectations, so I’d avoid it here.

Summary:
- For smallest flash with solid speed: stick to `-Os`
- For balanced size/perf if you can grow a bit: try `-O2`
- Skip `-O3`/`-Ofast` unless you’ve measured a clear win and verified correctness

